@voice ASKER storyteller pitch=+2.5 speed=1.05
RAVAN: Every year, you burn me. Ten heads. Fireworks inside. Millions of you, cheering.

RAVAN: But tell me... what if the arrow had missed?

ASKER: Wait. Ravan... won?

In one universe, yes. And this is his Lanka. In the year 2026.

Chapter one. The city of gold.

The Ramayan describes it as Swarna Lanka. A city of gold, built by Vishwakarma, the architect of the gods, on an island guarded by the ocean itself.

ASKER: But wasn't Ravan just... the villain?

Not just. In the stories, Ravan was a scholar. A master of the veena. A devotee of Shiva so intense, legend says he tried to lift Mount Kailash. The Shiva Tandav Stotram, still chanted today, is credited to him.

So imagine that mind... winning.

Chapter two. The empire.

By the year one thousand, Lanka controls every sea route between Africa and China. Spice, silk, gold. All of it pays a toll to one island.

And then, the Pushpak Vimaan. In the epic, a flying chariot that moves by thought. In this world, Lankan engineers spend two thousand years rebuilding it.

By 2026, Lanka has the busiest skyport on Earth. Silent golden aircraft rise from the palace like fireflies.

ASKER: Okay, that part sounds amazing.

And here's a real fact. In our world, Sri Lanka's first satellite, launched in 2019, was named after Ravan himself.

Ten great towers stand around the palace. One for each of Ravan's heads. Medicine. Music. Astronomy. War. Ten minds, ruling as one.

Chapter three. The shadow.

ASKER: So... where's the catch?

There is no Dussehra here. No festival where good defeats pride. Children grow up with a different story. The strong take what they want.

The ten towers watch everything. Disagree with the king, and you don't disappear. You're simply forgotten.

And somewhere in the archives, there is a story nobody is allowed to tell. Of a woman taken from a forest, who never got to go home.

ASKER: So was he good, or bad?

That's the real question. Ravan was brilliant. Powerful. Devoted. And it still wasn't enough. Even his own brother, Vibhishan, warned him, and walked away.

Maybe that's why the story needs him to lose. Not because he was weak. But because he was so strong, that only one flaw could bring him down.

Pride.

RAVAN: So you burn me every year...

RAVAN: ...so that you never become me.

So tell me. If Ravan had won, would you live in his golden Lanka? Comment yes, or no.
